Found by the pre-release audit 2026-11-13 (fidelity-auditor, leak7.pl).

Compiled code: $context->get_super($env,'c',$b_c,...) -- the block sub $b_<block> passes itself, so the Template that defines a block using super() forms a cycle and is never freed: ~52 kB per render_string, and per render with a noCache loader. Bounded with a caching loader (one Template per name).

Done when: a Template using super() is freed after render_string (regression test), suite green.

2026-11-17 main agent, re-diagnosed at HEAD 621ad19: cause confirmed, but the title and the done-criterion are wrong. The compiled source declares 'my ($root, $b_c)' and the block sub calls get_super(..., $b_c, ...), so it captures the variable that holds it. What leaks is NOT the Template object -- the count probe shows 0 Template, 0 Context, 0 Frame left per render -- it is the compiled code of the template (the block coderef and everything compiled with it), ~52 kB per render_string. CORRECTION to 'Done when': a test that weakens the Template passes today without any fix; the regression test must weaken the block coderef (the entry in the Template's blocks) and check that it is freed. Fix direction checked by runtime patch of the emitted source (repo untouched): first line of each block sub 'my $b_<name> = __SUB__;' so compile_super keeps emitting $b_<name> and now reads a per-call lexical; RSS growth reads 0. __SUB__ directly at the super() site is wrong: that site is often inside a nested callback sub. CORE::__SUB__ works without the feature in the eval'd source. No design decision needed; ready for the worker.

2026-11-17 06:18 worker: fix in the tree (uncommitted). compile_root (Compiler/Compiler.pm) emits 'my $b_<name> = CORE::__SUB__;' as the first statement of the body of every block sub (right after _emit_func_begin, before the frame push); compile_super is unchanged and its $b_<name> now reads that per-call lexical. The file-level $b_<name> is no longer captured by any sub. Decisions (worker's): (1) every block sub, not only blocks with super() -- upstream's 'function b_<name>' binds its own name in every block, no pre-scan that has to stay in sync with compile_super, cost measured at about 75 ns per block call; (2) CORE::__SUB__ in the emitted source rather than enabling the feature at the eval -- the source stays self-contained whoever evals it. Measured: get_super is today always emitted as the first statement of the block sub that owns the name (lift_super unshifts the Super node onto the block body; 15 hand-made shapes + 4 corpus sites, none in a nested callback), so __SUB__ at the site would also have been correct today; the per-call lexical was kept as directed because it does not rest on that transformer invariant. Both k109 cases in t/10-leaks.t pass; t/10-compiler.t and t/upstream/compiler.t stay green unchanged (neither asserts the emitted text of a block sub).

